L171: cite85†Blog L172: # Using the Gamepad API
L173: 
L174: Baseline
L175: 
L176: Widely available
L177: 
L178: *
L179: 
L180: This feature is well established and works across many devices and browser versions. It’s been available across browsers since March 2017.
L181: 
L182: * Some parts of this feature may have varying levels of support.
L183: 
L184:   * cite86†See full compatibility L185:   * cite87†Learn more L186: HTML provides the necessary components for rich, interactive game development. Technologies like `<canvas>`, WebGL, `<audio>`, and `<video>`, along with JavaScript implementations, support tasks that provide similar, if not the same, features as native code. The Gamepad API allows developers and designers to access and use gamepads and other game controllers.
L187: The cite88†Gamepad API introduces new events on the cite89†`Window` object for reading gamepad and controller (hereby referred to as gamepad) state. In addition to these events, the API also adds a cite90†`Gamepad` object, which you can use to query the state of a connected gamepad, and a cite91†`navigator.getGamepads()` method which you can use to get a list of gamepads known to the page.
L188: ## In this article
L189: 
L190:   * cite92†Connecting to a gamepad L191:   * cite93†Disconnecting a gamepad L192:   * cite94†Querying the Gamepad object L193:   * cite95†Complete example: Displaying gamepad state L194:   * cite96†Specifications L195:   * cite86†Browser compatibility L196: ## cite92†Connecting to a gamepad L197: 
L198: When a new gamepad is connected to the computer, the focused page first receives a cite97†`gamepadconnected` event. If a gamepad is already connected when the page loaded, the cite97†`gamepadconnected` event is dispatched to the focused page when the user presses a button or moves an axis.
L199: Note: In Firefox, gamepads are only exposed to a page when the user interacts with one with the page visible. This helps prevent gamepads from being used for cite98†fingerprinting the user. Once one gamepad has been interacted with, other gamepads that are connected will automatically be visible.
L200: 
L201: You can use cite97†`gamepadconnected` like this:
L202: js
L203: 
L204:     `window.addEventListener("gamepadconnected", (e) => {
L205:       console.log(
L206:         "Gamepad connected at index %d: %s. %d buttons, %d axes.",
L207:         e.gamepad.index,
L208:         e.gamepad.id,
L209:         e.gamepad.buttons.length,
L210:         e.gamepad.axes.length,
L211:       );
L212:     });
L213:     `
L214: 
L215: Each gamepad has a unique ID associated with it, which is available on the event's cite99†`gamepad` property.
L216: ## cite93†Disconnecting a gamepad L217: 
L218: When a gamepad is disconnected, and if a page has previously received data for that gamepad (e.g., cite97†`gamepadconnected` ), a second event is dispatched to the focused window, cite100†`gamepaddisconnected` :
L219: 
L220: js
L221: 
L222:     `window.addEventListener("gamepaddisconnected", (e) => {
L223:       console.log(
L224:         "Gamepad disconnected from index %d: %s",
L225:         e.gamepad.index,
L226:         e.gamepad.id,
L227:       );
L228:     });
L229:     `
L230: The gamepad's cite101†`index` property will be unique per-device connected to the system, even if multiple controllers of the same type are used. The `index` property also functions as the index into the cite102†`Array` returned by cite91†`Navigator.getGamepads()` .
L231: js
L232: 
L233:     `const gamepads = {};
L234: 
L235:     function gamepadHandler(event, connected) {
L236:       const gamepad = event.gamepad;
L237:       // Note: Use gamepad.index as the stable key, then read the latest
L238:       // state from navigator.getGamepads() inside your update loop.
L239: 
L240:       if (connected) {
L241:         gamepads[gamepad.index] = gamepad;
L242:       } else {
L243:         delete gamepads[gamepad.index];
L244:       }
L245:     }
L246: 
L247:     window.addEventListener("gamepadconnected", (e) => {
L248:       gamepadHandler(e, true);
L249:     });
L250:     window.addEventListener("gamepaddisconnected", (e) => {
L251:       gamepadHandler(e, false);
L252:     });
L253:     `
L254: This previous example shows how to keep track of which devices are connected by `index`. For current button and axis state, call cite91†`Navigator.getGamepads()` each frame and read the latest object for that `index`.
L255: ## cite94†Querying the Gamepad object L256: 
L257: As you can see, the gamepad events discussed above include a `gamepad` property on the event object, which returns a cite90†`Gamepad` object. We can use this to determine which gamepad (i.e., its ID) had caused the event, since multiple gamepads might be connected at once. To read the current button and axis state, use the gamepad's `index` and fetch the latest object from cite91†`Navigator.getGamepads()` in your animation loop.
L258: Performing such checks tends to involve using the cite90†`Gamepad` object in conjunction with an animation loop (e.g., cite103†`requestAnimationFrame` ), where developers want to make decisions for the current frame based on the state of the gamepad or gamepads.
L259: The cite91†`Navigator.getGamepads()` method returns an array of all devices currently visible to the webpage, as cite90†`Gamepad` objects (the first value is always `null`, so `null` will be returned if there are no gamepads connected.) This can then be used to get the same information. For example, the first code example above could be rewritten as shown below:
L260: js
L261: 
L262:     `window.addEventListener("gamepadconnected", (e) => {
L263:       const gp = navigator.getGamepads()[e.gamepad.index];
L264:       console.log(
L265:         "Gamepad connected at index %d: %s. %d buttons, %d axes.",
L266:         gp.index,
L267:         gp.id,
L268:         gp.buttons.length,
L269:         gp.axes.length,
L270:       );
L271:     });
L272:     `
L273: 
L274: The cite90†`Gamepad` object's properties are as follows:
L275:   * `id`: A string containing some information about the controller. This is not strictly specified, but in Firefox it will contain three pieces of information separated by dashes (`-`): two 4-digit hexadecimal strings containing the USB vendor and product id of the controller, and the name of the controller as provided by the driver. This information is intended to allow you to find a mapping for the controls on the device as well as display useful feedback to the user.
L276:   * `index`: An integer that is unique for each gamepad currently connected to the system. This can be used to distinguish multiple controllers. Note that disconnecting a device and then connecting a new device may reuse the previous index.
L277:   * `mapping`: A string indicating whether the browser has remapped the controls on the device to a known layout. Currently there is only one supported known layout — the cite104†standard gamepad†w3c.github.io . If the browser is able to map controls on the device to that layout the `mapping` property will be set to the string `standard`.
L278: 
L279:   * `connected`: A boolean indicating whether the gamepad is still connected to the system. If this is so the value is `True`; if not, it is `False`.
L280:   * `buttons`: An array of cite105†`GamepadButton` objects representing the buttons present on the device. Each cite105†`GamepadButton` has a `pressed` and a `value` property:
L281:     * The `pressed` property is a boolean indicating whether the button is currently pressed (`true`) or unpressed (`false`).
L282:     * The `value` property is a floating point value used to enable representing analog buttons, such as the triggers on many modern gamepads. The values are normalized to the range 0.0..1.0, with 0.0 representing a button that is not pressed, and 1.0 representing a button that is fully pressed.
L283:   * `axes`: An array representing the controls with axes present on the device (e.g., analog thumb sticks). Each entry in the array is a floating point value in the range -1.0 - 1.0, representing the axis position from the lowest value (-1.0) to the highest value (1.0).
L284:   * `timestamp`: This returns a cite106†`DOMHighResTimeStamp` representing the last time the data for this gamepad was updated, allowing developers to determine if the `axes` and `button` data have been updated from the hardware. The value must be relative to the `navigationStart` attribute of the cite107†`PerformanceTiming` interface.
L285: Values are monotonically increasing, meaning that they can be compared to determine the ordering of updates, as newer values will always be greater than or equal to older values. Note that this property is not currently supported in Firefox.
L286: Note: The Gamepad object is available on the cite97†`gamepadconnected` event rather than the cite89†`Window` object itself, for security reasons. You can also access gamepads through cite91†`Navigator.getGamepads()` . In practice, you should poll cite91†`Navigator.getGamepads()` and read the current object for a known `index` each frame, rather than relying on a long-lived reference from an earlier event.
L287: ### cite108†Using button information L288: 
L289: Let's look at an example that displays connection information for one gamepad (it ignores subsequent gamepad connections) and allows you to move a ball around the screen using the four gamepad buttons on the right-hand side of the gamepad. You can cite109†view the demo live†chrisdavidmills.github.io , and cite110†find the source code†github.com on GitHub.
L290: To start with, we declare some variables: The `gamepadInfo` paragraph that the connection info is written into, the `ball` that we want to move, the `start` variable that acts as the ID for `requestAnimation Frame`, the `a` and `b` variables that act as position modifiers for moving the ball, and the shorthand variables that will be used for the cite103†`requestAnimationFrame()` and cite111†`cancelAnimationFrame()` cross browser forks.
L291: js
L292: 
L293:     `const gamepadInfo = document.getElementById("gamepad-info");
L294:     const ball = document.getElementById("ball");
L295:     let start;
L296:     let a = 0;
L297:     let b = 0;
L298:     `
L299: 
L300: Next we use the cite97†`gamepadconnected` event to check for a gamepad being connected. When one is connected, we grab the gamepad using cite91†`navigator.getGamepads()[0]` , print information about the gamepad into our gamepad info `div`, and fire the `gameLoop()` function that starts the whole ball movement process up.
L301: js
L302: 
L303:     `window.addEventListener("gamepadconnected", (e) => {
L304:       const gp = navigator.getGamepads()[e.gamepad.index];
L305:       gamepadInfo.textContent = `Gamepad connected at index ${gp.index}: ${gp.id}. It has ${gp.buttons.length} buttons and ${gp.axes.length} axes.`;
L306: 
L307:       gameLoop();
L308:     });
L309:     `
L310: Now we use the cite100†`gamepaddisconnected` event to check if the gamepad is disconnected again. If so, we stop the cite103†`requestAnimationFrame()` loop (see below) and revert the gamepad information back to what it was originally.
L311: 
L312: js
L313: 
L314:     `window.addEventListener("gamepaddisconnected", (e) => {
L315:       gamepadInfo.textContent = "Waiting for gamepad.";
L316: 
L317:       cancelAnimationFrame(start);
L318:     });
L319:     `
L320: Now on to the main game loop. In each execution of the loop we check if one of four buttons is being pressed; if so, we update the values of the `a` and `b` movement variables appropriately, then update the cite112†`left` and cite113†`top` properties, changing their values to the current values of `a` and `b` respectively. This has the effect of moving the ball around the screen.
L321: 
L322: After all this is done, we use our `requestAnimationFrame()` to request the next animation frame, running `gameLoop()` again.
L323: js
L324: 
L325:     `function gameLoop() {
L326:       const gamepads = navigator.getGamepads();
L327:       if (!gamepads) {
L328:         return;
L329:       }
L330: 
L331:       const gp = gamepads[0];
L332:       if (gp.buttons[0].pressed) {
L333:         b--;
L334:       }
L335:       if (gp.buttons[2].pressed) {
L336:         b++;
L337:       }
L338:       if (gp.buttons[1].pressed) {
L339:         a++;
L340:       }
L341:       if (gp.buttons[3].pressed) {
L342:         a--;
L343:       }
L344: 
L345:       ball.style.left = `${a * 2}px`;
L346:       ball.style.top = `${b * 2}px`;
L347:       start = requestAnimationFrame(gameLoop);
L348:     }
L349:     `
L350: ## cite95†Complete example: Displaying gamepad state L351: 
L352: This example shows how to use the cite90†`Gamepad` object, as well as the cite97†`gamepadconnected` and cite100†`gamepaddisconnected` events to display the state of all gamepads connected to the system. The example is based on a cite114†Gamepad demo†luser.github.io , which has the cite115†source code available on GitHub†github.com .
L353: js
L354: 
L355:     `let loopStarted = false;
L356: 
L357:     window.addEventListener("gamepadconnected", (evt) => {
L358:       addGamepad(evt.gamepad);
L359:     });
L360:     window.addEventListener("gamepaddisconnected", (evt) => {
L361:       removeGamepad(evt.gamepad);
L362:     });
L363: 
L364:     function addGamepad(gamepad) {
L365:       const d = document.createElement("div");
L366:       d.setAttribute("id", `controller${gamepad.index}`);
L367: 
L368:       const t = document.createElement("h1");
L369:       t.textContent = `gamepad: ${gamepad.id}`;
L370:       d.append(t);
L371: 
L372:       const b = document.createElement("ul");
L373:       b.className = "buttons";
L374:       gamepad.buttons.forEach((button, i) => {
L375:         const e = document.createElement("li");
L376:         e.className = "button";
L377:         e.textContent = `Button ${i}`;
L378:         b.append(e);
L379:       });
L380: 
L381:       d.append(b);
L382: 
L383:       const a = document.createElement("div");
L384:       a.className = "axes";
L385: 
L386:       gamepad.axes.forEach((axis, i) => {
L387:         const p = document.createElement("progress");
L388:         p.className = "axis";
L389:         p.setAttribute("max", "2");
L390:         p.setAttribute("value", "1");
L391:         p.textContent = i;
L392:         a.append(p);
L393:       });
L394: 
L395:       d.appendChild(a);
L396: 
L397:       // See https://github.com/luser/gamepadtest/blob/master/index.html
L398:       const start = document.querySelector("#start");
L399:       if (start) {
L400:         start.style.display = "none";
L401:       }
L402: 
L403:       document.body.append(d);
L404:       if (!loopStarted) {
L405:         requestAnimationFrame(updateStatus);
L406:         loopStarted = true;
L407:       }
L408:     }
L409: 
L410:     function removeGamepad(gamepad) {
L411:       document.querySelector(`#controller${gamepad.index}`).remove();
L412:     }
L413: 
L414:     function updateStatus() {
L415:       for (const gamepad of navigator.getGamepads()) {
L416:         if (!gamepad) continue;
L417: 
L418:         const d = document.getElementById(`controller${gamepad.index}`);
L419:         const buttonElements = d.getElementsByClassName("button");
L420: 
L421:         for (const [i, button] of gamepad.buttons.entries()) {
L422:           const el = buttonElements[i];
L423: 
L424:           const pct = `${Math.round(button.value * 100)}%`;
L425:           el.style.backgroundSize = `${pct} ${pct}`;
L426:           if (button.pressed) {
L427:             el.textContent = `Button ${i} [PRESSED]`;
L428:             el.style.color = "#42f593";
L429:             el.className = "button pressed";
L430:           } else {
L431:             el.textContent = `Button ${i}`;
L432:             el.style.color = "#2e2d33";
L433:             el.className = "button";
L434:           }
L435:         }
L436:         const axisElements = d.getElementsByClassName("axis");
L437:         for (const [i, axis] of gamepad.axes.entries()) {
L438:           const el = axisElements[i];
L439:           el.textContent = `${i}: ${axis.toFixed(4)}`;
L440:           el.setAttribute("value", axis + 1);
L441:         }
L442:       }
L443: 
L444:       requestAnimationFrame(updateStatus);
L445:     }
L446:     `
